1. What is service container
    - it is a tool that manages class dependencies and automatically resolves them, allowing for dependency injection
    and loose coupling.
    this tool solves such problems as tight coupling, poor testability
    it works in the following way: container checks the type in a constructor for example, then finds that class,
    resolves it and passes it back to the constructor.

2. Bind vs singleton
    - bind creates a new instance on every resolve, while singleton creates one instance shared everywhere for the entire
    application lifecycle.
    bind is good for stateless, low-weight objects, and when u don't need the state.
    singleton is used if the object creation is pretty expensive, when we need the only state through the whole app
    lifecycle.

3. Interface binding
    - it is the process of mapping an interface to a concrete implementation in the service container, so laravel
    knows which class to resolve.
    in general, use of abstractions as class dependencies is needed for realization of dependency inversion principle.
    it gives loose coupling, flexibility, testability.

4. Service providers
    - it is a central place where u configure and register bindings, services and application bootstrapping logic in
    laravel.
    it has two methods: register() and boot()
    register is used to bind things into the service container, so here we configure dependencies
    boot is used to run code after all services have been registered.

5. $this->app->instance()
    - it registers a specific object instance in laravel service container. it is commonly used in tests to replace
    real services with mocks or fakes.
    $this->app->instance(MinioService::class, $mock);